WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:10.940
In 2017, the administrative agencies of the
federal government—which many call the administrative

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.580
state-- issued over 3000 rules and regulations.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:20.170
That sounds like a lot, but they have done
that every year since 1993—a total of over

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.960
100,000 rules.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.900
Complying with all these rules is estimated
to cost $2 trillion each year—more than

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.460
the total taxes paid annually by US businesses
and individuals.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:37.460
Congress—and now President Trump--have tried
to stanch this flow, but with little success.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:42.050
These efforts have failed because the courts
have not done what the Framers of the Constitution

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.020
expected
They were supposed to narrowly interpret the

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:50.960
laws that give agencies their rulemaking authority
But for many years the courts have allowed

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.300
the agencies to interpret the scope of their
own powers.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.339
The Supreme Court, with a conservative majority,
will have an opportunity to change this system

00:01:00.339 --> 00:01:02.879
and rein in the administrative state.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.700
The question is whether, after years of neglect,
it will take this important step.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.840
To learn more about my take on the administrative state,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.840
check out the link to my book "Judicial Fortitude" in the description below.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.380
And be sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

